00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:04.710 align:start position:0%
 
i've always  been interested in working on systems  when i'm talking about systems what i  mean is that often  we talk about issues as sort of siloed  things  climate's over here poverty is over here  access to higher education is over here  but the fact is  that all of those problems they all  exist sort of like within  the same system and a lot of times  those systems intersect every time  you change a policy you are changing a  system every time rihanna gun wright  wants to tell you that the world is more  connected  than you might think she's the director  of climate policy at the roosevelt  institute  and one of the architects of the green  new deal the sweeping comprehensive  climate legislation  championed by alexandria ocasio-cortez  and met by political opponents with  let's say some criticism after reading  the green new deal  i'm mostly afraid of not being able to  get through this speech  with a straight face the green new deal  is expansive it's comprehensive  so how does a woman with fewer than  three years of experience in the world  of climate justice  end up writing one of the most  controversial and impactful climate  bills in american history  because she saw that everything in  american power is connected  and she wants everyone else to see it  with her  [Music]  born and raised in chicago rihanna gun  wright grew up with asthma  and i had asthma to the point that by  the time i think i was in like  third or fourth grade i was out for like  a week every spring  i would get sick and i would be out of  school  she didn't make the connection at the  time but we now know today that her  asthma  like many others was most likely linked  to the air pollution in her neighborhood  where she grew up  the situation for a lot of other kids  was that their parents worked hourly  jobs right  so every time your child is out of  school not only are you hurting their  educational outcomes  you have mothers trying to figure out  what to do about child care  these things are all connected  that connection has not only shaped gun  rights world view  but the fundamental tenets of the green  new deal  a lot of what the green new deal did was  just  connect a lot of conversations that were  happening already  climate justice and environmental  justice activists have been talking  about how  environment is larger than polar bears  and ice caps in the arctic  so it was just essentially trying to  create a policy proposal that not just  met the scale of the problem but  actually tried to address the problem  as it exists in the real world which is  a mass of overlapping  and interconnected issues the green new  deal is a 14-page document and it covers  a lot of ground it not only promises to  get carbon emissions to net zero by 2050  but to add millions of green jobs and  makes the link that climate issues like  fossil fuel emissions  are justice issues since people of color  are more likely to live near power  plants and other polluting factories  and they suffer higher levels of asthma  and greater risk of early death  from air pollution  while rihanna's writing of the green new  deal may be one of the most influential  documents in the green space  she's a relative newcomer when it comes  to climate justice  for her she sees that as an asset  instead of a hindrance  when we talk about climate change most  times people are talking about super  scientific stuff  carbon footprints parts per million how  much carbon is in the atmosphere  and there's this feeling i feel like  often in climate policy that you if you  don't have a background on those things  you can't talk about  it but the fact is that most people  experience  climate change as economic effects and  health effects  they experience it as hurricane katrina  they experience it as the wildfires  in paradise they experience it as this  heat wave where they don't have money to  afford air conditioning  climate change is not a partisan thing  so rihanna took this pragmatic approach  all throughout her career originally she  was a policy analyst for the detroit  health department  and detroit is a hotbed for  environmental injustice  when i was working there there was an  incinerator in the middle of the city  and around that area asthma rates were  like three times  the state average residents were  complaining that they couldn't go  outside because it smelled so bad  so trying to figure out what to do about  it it just became clear that these  things had  health effects but the reasons that it  was happening were beyond what we could  do for people  so after working on abdul syed's 2018  michigan gubernatorial campaign and  losing  rihanna was looking for a job so she  emailed congresswoman alexandria  ocasio-cortez's campaign manager  looking for something short-term and  when she was offered a job at new  consensus a progressive think  tank they're like hey so we are  developing a green new deal  which is a mobilization on climate to  restructure the us economy and reduce  emissions  in addition the idea was to create the  lofty goal  of zero waste zero poverty and zero  racial wealth gap so they were like oh  so  you can write this whole thing in like  four months right  and i was like no but maybe if you give  me two research assistants  we can try uh and so  that's what we did  no one does economic mobilizations  anymore in the u.s the last time we did  that was in world war  ii so the first thing we had to figure  out was like  how do you actually run an industrial  mobilization  what does that look like that's when  rihanna brought in  other organizations to not only pitch  ideas for what the green new deal should  look like  but how to shift power structures and  figure out how money should be dispersed  so i work with a green new deal network  which is essentially  a coalition of movements but  what became clear was the folks who had  been put together  were not climate justice organizers or  advocates  that's john washington he's part of the  green new deal network  and he's worked with rihanna during his  time at people united for sustainable  housing  or push initially he had  doubts about rihanna's new deal  coalition they were folks who had had  success like rihanna in electoral work  but who had never done climate justice  work and so  rihanna recognized that there was a gap  in that and really took  it all in and built a relationship with  myself and  many others across the climate justice  movement  the only way we're going to figure out  how to do such big  intersectional policy is to have people  from various  not only viewpoints and expertise but  people  that are going to be affected by this in  the room talking to each other building  relationships and working together  one of those people she listened to is  lucy velez she lives in buffalo  and she's a climate justice activist  while her story is only tangentially  related to an environmental issue  it's actually addressing the knock-on  effects that spill over  into different but connected areas of  life  i thought you know my cough was just a  cough all these  uh congestion issues and colds were just  things that  normally you know most people experience  like rihanna lucy suffered from  persistent medical issues from pollution  most likely caused by the peace bridge  connecting buffalo to canada  which emitted emissions into the  surrounding neighborhoods  in the year 98 i got a house and  i thought that that house was in good  condition  but in 2016 i was  hospitalized with like a lung and brown  kill infection  and when lucy returned from the hospital  i went into a room  which happened to be my office and there  was this black  fuzzy mold  that's when members of push knocked on  lucy's door one day  and they were able to help her fix her  house it took a while to find different  funding sources to add up to the amount  of money it was required to fix lucy's  home  because it was in pretty bad shape the  cost to my house  was forty thousand dollars to cover  the things that i needed so lucy got her  home fixed  and then a few months later she gets a  thing from keybank saying that they're  redoing her mortgage  because the value of her home has gone  up her mortgage payment was doubled  i didn't have the money i couldn't find  the money to pay for  you know that kind of that kind of jump  and so she used part of her burial fund  to pay her taxes so she could keep her  house  that's the problem with lucy's situation  it started as a pollution related issue  and that issue ended up affecting her  mortgage  that domino effect is what the green new  deal is trying to address  that's what einstein said right the  consciousness that created a problem is  not the consciousness that's going to  solve it  so the consciousness of the non-profit  and private world is not what's going to  solve the problem it's the consciousness  of the community  and so i think the green new deal  network and the team have started really  to  to to address that they've started to  build a national network of  organizations that do this work  and that understand what people in  communities are experiencing  [Music]  today's biggest issue with  interconnected consequences  for a fourth straight day the  coronavirus claimed more than 1 000  lives in this country  infections are also still surging but  with covid spreading throughout the  united states  it seems that climate issues including  the green new deal  are taking a back seat to the pandemic  but that hasn't stopped opponents  making their feelings known what does  placing emissions standards on  airlines have to do with getting iowa  families and workers  the relief they need right now we're  here trying to fight for the man and  woman on the street in our hometowns  and yet they're fighting for the green  new deal  but rihanna says that covid and climate  justice have one major thing in common  they both ravage poor communities of  color and it's not a coincidence  if you look at the hot spots of covet  deaths  they basically mapped on almost directly  onto front line communities  one of the reasons why covet ravaged  communities of color was because we put  toxic pollution there and now  covet is taking them out at double the  [Applause]  rates  with joe biden's 1.7 trillion dollar  green initiative plan  some are calling it his own spin on the  green new deal  so it's catching on in the mainstream  but rihanna still knows she has a long  hard fight ahead of her  because ultimately changing an entire  system  means getting citizens to realize their  own power  over that system we ran into a lot of  people who would talk about  how they felt powerless  [Music]  but they always had power in some  measure  and it's just really important to  remember  that you have the right to gather with  other people  [Music]  we all have the ability to emotionally  affect another person to make another  person feel connected  and that is powerful  [Music]

